{
    "_comment": [
        "SHARED FIXTURE - consumed by BOTH repos. Edits must keep both suites green.",
        "  frontend: src/features/trackers/utils/kpi-parity.test.ts  (kpi-utils.ts + kpi-adapter.ts)",
        "  backend:  tests/test_habit_stats.py                       (services/habit_stats.py)",
        "The backend locates this file via the KPI_PARITY_CASES env var, defaulting to",
        "../habit-tracker-front-end/src/test-support/kpi-parity-cases.json relative to the backend repo root.",
        "",
        "Every case carries an explicit anchor_date (the 'today' the math is relative to).",
        "Neither suite may read the real clock: the backend passes anchor_date as `today`,",
        "the frontend pins it with vi.setSystemTime because kpi-utils calls new Date() internally.",
        "",
        "`expected` holds quantities the two implementations AGREE on - assert on both sides.",
        "`divergent` holds quantities where they currently DISAGREE. Each side asserts its own",
        "recorded value so drift still fails, and `bugs` names the defect (see _divergences).",
        "When a divergence is fixed, move the quantity from `divergent` into `expected`.",
        "",
        "Values were derived by running each implementation, not written by hand.",
        "habit.created_date is an ISO datetime; tracker.status is TrackerStatus",
        "(0 = not completed, 1 = skipped, 2 = completed)."
    ],
    "_divergences": {
        "longest-streak-end-date-tie-break": "longest_streak_end_date breaks a tie between equal-length streaks in opposite directions. Backend calculate_kpis uses `>=` so the MOST RECENT streak wins ('better for a recency label', per its comment). Frontend kpi-adapter's reduce uses `s.length > max.length` so the EARLIEST wins. The backend is right: the field feeds a recency sublabel ('N days - Mon'), so an optimistic toggle currently jumps that date backwards in time and snaps forward on refetch."
    },
